    <p>The diagram above shows the three primary subsystems. The <strong>Rule Library</strong> is the source of truth for domain rules and the compact <code>RULES_INDEX.md</code> that models use during discovery. The <strong>Protocol</strong> (<code>AGENTS.md</code>) defines the loading contract. The <strong>Eval Benchmark</strong> orchestrates fixture runs through the CoCo CLI, dispatches prompts to frontier models via Snowflake Cortex, scores each response against the protocol contract, and writes structured artifacts to <code>results/</code>. The <strong>Report Generator</strong> reads those artifacts and produces this document.</p>

    <h3>Part 2: A Single Benchmark Run</h3>

    <p>When <code>ai-rules rule-loader eval</code> is invoked, the harness executes each test fixture against the target model one or more times (<code>--runs</code>). Each fixture is a synthetic user prompt constructed to trigger a specific set of rule-loading paths&mdash;for example, a Python ETL task is expected to load <code>200-python-core.md</code>, while a Snowflake query prompt should load <code>100-snowflake-core.md</code>. The fixture definition includes the expected rules, any rules that must <em>not</em> be loaded, and dependency relationships between rules.</p>

    <p>The runner dispatches each fixture to the model as a conversational turn with the <code>AGENTS.md</code> bootstrap as the system prompt. It captures the full tool-call trace across all turns until the model produces a final response. That response and trace are then passed to the scoring engine, which checks compliance across several dimensions before writing results to disk.</p>

