    <h3>Test Fixtures</h3>

    <p>Fixtures are the fundamental unit of measurement. Each fixture is a synthetic user prompt designed to exercise a specific rule-loading path. A fixture specifies the prompt text, the set of rules the model <em>must</em> load (<code>required</code>), rules it must <em>not</em> load (<code>forbidden</code>), and dependency relationships between rules (if rule A is loaded, rule B must also be present). Fixtures are organized into three complexity tiers:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Simple:</strong> Single-domain prompts that should trigger exactly one or two rules. Low ambiguity, high determinism. Used to establish a baseline and detect direct regressions from rule changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Medium:</strong> Multi-domain prompts where the correct rule set is non-obvious. Tests the model's ability to correctly classify the task and load rules from more than one domain without over-loading.</li>
      <li><strong>Complex:</strong> Multi-step tasks that span several domains and may involve dependent rules, forbidden rule exclusions, and structured output requirements. These fixtures most closely reflect real-world agentic workloads and are where stochastic instability (FM-5) is most commonly observed.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Each fixture is run multiple times per model (<code>--runs</code>, default 5) to distinguish stable compliance from lucky passes. A model that passes a fixture only 2 out of 5 times is flagged as stochastically unstable—the rule loading is sensitive to sampling variance, which is a reliability risk in production even if the aggregate pass rate looks acceptable.</p>

    <h3>Output Artifacts</h3>

    <p>Every invocation of the eval harness streams results to a timestamped run directory under <code>results/</code>. The artifacts are written incrementally as each fixture completes, so a partial run is always recoverable. The full artifact set for a completed run is:</p>

    <dl style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap:0.4rem 1.2rem; font-size:0.9rem;">
      <dt><code>manifest.json</code></dt>
      <dd>Run-level metadata written once per invocation: model name, number of runs, fixture count, fixture selection, concurrency setting, git branch and SHA, eval harness version, and a per-pass status index with timestamps and pass/fail totals. This is the primary key for report discovery&mdash;the report generator uses it to identify the latest run per model.</dd>

      <dt><code>run-NN/run_meta.json</code></dt>
      <dd>Per-pass metadata for pass number <code>NN</code>. Records which fixtures started and completed within that pass, their individual start/end timestamps, and pass/fail status. Used to compute per-pass statistics and detect partially completed passes from interrupted runs.</dd>

      <dt><code>run-NN/fixtures/&lt;fixture-id&gt;.json</code></dt>
      <dd>The primary scoring artifact. Contains the full match result (<code>missing_required</code>, <code>extra_loaded</code>, <code>missing_dependencies</code>, <code>forbidden_present</code>), the <code>signal_report</code> with per-failure-mode signals (including <code>cited_without_read</code> for FM-1 detection), citation drift list, token counts, cost, turn count, duration, and the model's final response text. This is the file consumed by the failure mode heatmap and per-fixture results matrix.</dd>

      <dt><code>run-NN/fixtures/&lt;fixture-id&gt;.transcript.jsonl</code></dt>
      <dd>The full tool-call trace as a newline-delimited JSON stream. Each line is a turn event with a sequence number, timestamp, event kind (<code>tool_call</code>, <code>tool_result</code>, <code>assistant_text</code>), and event detail. This is the ground truth for FM-1 detection: the scorer cross-references <code>cited_without_read</code> against this trace to confirm whether a cited rule file was actually opened via <code>read_file</code> before the model declared it loaded.</dd>

      <dt><code>run-NN/summary.json</code> &amp; <code>summary.json</code></dt>
      <dd>Aggregate pass counts and totals for a single pass (<code>run-NN/summary.json</code>) and across all passes (<code>summary.json</code> at the run root). These rolled-up totals are what the report generator reads to compute the per-model pass rates displayed in the bar chart and performance table.</dd>
    </dl>

    <details>
      <summary style="cursor:pointer; font-weight:600; margin-top:1.5rem;">Glossary</summary>
      <ul style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Fixture:</strong> A synthetic user prompt with an explicit expected rule set, used as the unit of measurement in the eval benchmark.</li>
        <li><strong>Run directory:</strong> A timestamped folder under <code>results/</code> containing all artifacts for one invocation of the eval harness against one model.</li>
        <li><strong>Pass:</strong> One complete sweep through all fixtures. A run with <code>--runs 5</code> contains five passes (<code>run-01</code> through <code>run-05</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Signal report:</strong> A structured summary of per-failure-mode indicators extracted from the tool trace and response text for a single fixture run.</li>
        <li><strong>Stochastic instability:</strong> A fixture that produces mixed pass/fail results across runs due to sampling variance in the model&rsquo;s output.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>
